  finding: >-
    ui/src/Spinner.tsx is a new surface that no design answers for, and the gate is right to refuse it. It
    is worse than unaccounted: it CONTRADICTS the receipted decision of the very design this WU cites.
    Design run 11, the artifact named in implements above, receipts motion-informs as "satisfied - nothing
    animates, and nothing moves" (line 1277). Design run 10, which run 11 superseded, states the same
    decision with its reason attached: "nothing animates; in-flight is a disabled control plus a static
    word, never a spinner or a bar" (line 563), and again in its stage log - "Button's @states fork D4 ·
    motion-informs (the reason it is a word and not a spinner)" (line 672). The in-flight half of Button's
    fork D4 was therefore designed AS A WORD, deliberately, against a named rule, twice. This commit adds
    a turning ring in three places - the header slot, the import ask, and the menu item of the running act
    - which is precisely the channel both runs rejected. An earlier draft of this note argued the ring was
    additive chrome inside an unchanged layout, citing measurements showing no zone moved. Those
    measurements are true and they are beside the point: motion-informs is not a layout rule, and a design
    receipt that says nothing animates is not satisfied by animation that does not displace anything.
    Two smaller items ride the same gap and are named so they are not rediscovered: the glyph column added
    to the menu items, and the one newly minted face on it (the erase glyph; the other two reuse
    IconButton's save glyph and the mark FlowList already prints on an imported row).
  note: >-
